foreign  it's estimated that seat belts have  collectively saved over 1 Million Lives  making them one of the most cost  effective Public Health inventions ever  made these incredible devices can reduce  fatalities in car accidents by about  half yes their development and  implementation as standard equipment was  surprisingly Rocky for something that  seems so straightforward The Humble seat  belt is more sophisticated than you  might think  even though wearing a restraint in a  vehicle moving 100 kilometers per hour  may seem like common sense to us today  it took many years to convince the  driving public to buckle up a problem  that persists in many countries around  the world this ingenious device saves  lives and this is how it does it  [Music]  in 1903 race car driver Bernie Oldfield  became the first person to drive over  100 kilometers per hour he was all too  aware of the dangers he was subjecting  himself to a commissioned Leslie Leroy  Irvin another Daredevil of the 1920s to  develop a harness for his racing cars  Irvin had developed his own static line  parachute to jump out of airplanes and  in 1919 became the first American to  jump out of a plane and manually deploy  a parachute from a backpack in midair  the harness they came up with did not  catch on however despite its life-saving  potential it had a number of problems  first they were simply uncomfortable  which discouraged drivers and passengers  from wearing them then there was the  very real problem of seat belt syndrome  injuries directly related to wearing a  seat belt during a collision early seat  belts only went across the lap in the  event of a crash this rigid single  pointer restraint laying in line with  vital organs and the lumbar spine could  cause severe injuries  the prevailing thought at the time was  that it would be better to be ejected  from the vehicle which might catch on  fire or go underwater than to remain  inside of it during a crash by being  stopped forcefully by a thin piece of  fabric  despite Rising fatalities and injuries  during the rise of the automobile from  the 1920s and 1950s the general public  showed little interest in wearing seat  belts even though Nash Motors installed  seat belts in 40 000 cars only about 1  000 of them were actually used by  drivers when Ford led a marketing  campaign in 1955 to promote seat belts  and other safety options they Too Faced  public backlash however in the 1950s  resistance began to ebb away with a new  innovation by Swedish engineer Niels  Bolin Bolin first began developing  features for Saab including an injection  seats bus was then poached by the  president of Volvo he experimented with  several designs including a seat belt  that fastened between the legs an idea  which 95 of my audience just gasped at  he ultimately settled on the design  we're familiar with today the  three-point harness this setup more  evenly distributes the forces of a crash  across the shoulders and hips  Olin was able to prove the effectiveness  of this design and received the U.S  patent force in 1962 but rather than  profit from it the design was released  for free Paving the way for a new and  Incredibly important safety feature  while certain elements of the seat belt  have been modified and improved over  time the main design Remains the Same we  need a way for the strap itself to wind  and unwind under normal circumstances  allowing passengers to easily move  around and take it on and off without  adjusting its size for different  passengers and drivers we have all  experienced that annoying phenomenon  where you try to put on a seat belt too  quickly causing it to jam this happens  as a result of a false alarm in the seat  belt mechanism this mechanism it has a  number of components the simplest being  the spiral torsion spring when the seat  belt is Unwound it spins a cylinder at  the center of this torsion spring  pulling the spring into the center into  a compressed State storing energy that  can retract the seat belt tightly  against your body once the belt buckle  is locked into the Buckle socket this  mechanism is usually attached to one  side of the webbing spool while the  locking mechanism that will prevent the  spool from being Unwound at all during a  crash is located on the other side for  this mechanism to work we need a way to  detect a crash extremely quickly one  kind of locking mechanism reacts to the  momentum change of the vehicle just as  our bodies want to continue moving  forward in a crash a weighted mechanism  inside the seat belt will too first we  need a ratchet gear like this attached  to the same cylinder at the center of  our torsion spring this gear will be  engaged to prevent rotation now we need  a mechanism that can engage this gear  when the car decelerates this can be  done with a weighted pendulum when the  car decelerates this weight will swing  forward causing its opposite end to jam  into the ratchet preventing it from  rotating any further like any good  safety mechanism there is a redundancy  in its design being backed up by another  locking mechanism one you can test  without crashing your car by tucking on  your seatbelt hard enough this mechanism  will activate as it depends on  centrifugal force to activate two  weighted levers are attached to the  central spool when the central spool  unwinds at a normal speed these levers  do not create enough Force to overcome  the force of a spring that pushes them  towards the center but if the webbing  comes out in a sudden jerk the  centrifugal force exceeds the force of  the spring and the levers push outwards  engaging the Locking teeth and  preventing further rotation there is  another safety mechanism added to make  seat belts even safer when driving we  often slump forward slightly leaving  slack between our body and the seas in  the event of a crash it's best if we are  tightly held against the Seas so there  is a mechanism within the seat belts  that actually forcefully pulls us  against the seat during a crash this  mechanism is fired electronically by the  same sensor that initiates the explosion  that expands airbags this mechanism is  also fired by an explosive charge it's  called the pre-tensioner a chamber at  the bottom of the device has two  electrodes an igniter material and a  piston when the car's Central processor  detects the Collision it applies an  electric current that ignites the  explosive and the sudden burst of  pressure forces a mechanism to actuase  to quickly retract the seat belt and  pull the passenger tightly against the  Seas finally the seat belt has several  mechanisms built in to reduce the risk  of seat belt syndrome injuries some  webbings have a fold sewn into them with  stitches that will break when a set  amount of force is applied allowing the  person to move forward a little bit more  there is also the torsion bar a length  of metal attack to the rotating spool  this torsion barrier is designed to  twist and absorb some of the forests  from a crash instead of allowing the  energy to be transferred to the  passenger's body  despite the clear benefits of this  three-point belt system it took decades  before the use of seat belts really  became widespread although Wisconsin was  the first state to require all carers to  install seat belts in the front in 1961  it wasn't until 1984 that another state  New York required passengers to actually  wear them I remember growing up in  Ireland in the 90s and seeing this  change in opinions about seat belts in  real time and I especially remember  moving to Malaysia a country with a  terrible road safety record and where a  friend of mine died because he wasn't  wearing a seatbelt and arguing with  local co-workers who refused to wear  them the national highway traffic safety  administration estimates fifteen  thousand lives are saved by seat belts  every year in the us alone so please put  on your seatbelt they do save lives  of course there are plenty of other  devices helping to make Cara safer we  have front and side airbags crumple  zones driver assist and emergency  braking and Volvo is now proposing a  blanket-style seat belt for its  autonomous carriers that would keep  passengers safe and comfortable even  when they're napping but that's still  something of a pipe dream since we're  still a long way from self-driving cars  for now we are stuck with the  three-point harness a simple and cheap  device to keep passengers safe sometimes  Simple Solutions really are the most  effective other times more complicated  Solutions are needed one concept is so  complicated that I struggle to wrap my  head around this I have a basic  understanding of how computers work and  a firm grasp of physics but the physics  of quantum Computing goes right over my  head I wanted to start learning more  about it in preparation for a future  video but my usual method of using  college textbooks was not helping me I  didn't know what I didn't know which  makes asking the right questions really  hard  then I discovered that brilliant has a  new course on Quantum Computing created  in partnership with Quantum Computing  researchers  it took me from the very basics of  quantum Computing starting at a level I  understand with basic logic gates  testing my understanding along the way  and explaining to me the correct answers  in clear understandable terms when I got  something wrong I regularly have to dive  into the deep end of unfamiliar topics  to write the scripts for this Channel  and the most difficult part is usually  knowing what questions to ask but  brilliant removes that challenge with  curated guided courses designed to  gradually level up your knowledge  you can take this course or one of  brilliant's many other courses including  her very own partnered course  by signing up to brilliant's free trial  with the link in the description or by  going to brilliant.org forward slash  real engineering and if you found it  valuable which I'm sure you will you can  continue 20 off the annual premium  subscription  this offer is only available to the  first 500 people to sign up brilliant  teachers with interesting and fun  interactive courses that challenge you  along the way making the learning  process much easier and more rewarding  and they're adding more content monthly  so there's always something new to learn  you can get started for free by clicking  the link on screen right now and the  first 500 people to do so will get 20  off Brilliance annual premium  subscription

